S.push(H1('S2. Proof of Proposition 1'));
S.push(P0(`If ε has distribution *F* not depending on *s*, the warning is correct if and only if *a* ≤ ε + *s* ≤ *b*, that is, ε ∈ [*a* − *s*, *b* − *s*], an interval of width *w* = *b* − *a*. Hence *P*(*s*) = *F*(*b* − *s*) − *F*(*a* − *s*) ≤ sup_{x} [*F*(*x* + *w*) − *F*(*x*)] = *C*_{w}(*F*), which is part (i).`));
S.push(P0(`For part (ii), let *f* be the density, unimodal and symmetric about *m*, and write *g*(*x*) = *F*(*x* + *w*) − *F*(*x*). Then *g*′(*x*) = *f*(*x* + *w*) − *f*(*x*). For *x* < *m* − *w*/2 the point *x* + *w* is closer to *m* than *x*, so *f*(*x* + *w*) ≥ *f*(*x*) and *g* is non-decreasing; for *x* > *m* − *w*/2 the reverse holds. Therefore *g* is maximal at *x* = *m* − *w*/2, and *g*(*m* − *w*/2) = Pr(|ε − *m*| ≤ *w*/2). The interval [*a* − *s*, *b* − *s*] is centred at *m* when *s*^{*} = (*a* + *b*)/2 − *m*; for [−13, +2] this is −5.5 − *m*. ∎`));
S.push(P0(`The proposition concerns a fixed distribution. In the sequential policy, a different shift moves the alarm to a different cycle, where the unshifted error has a different distribution; Section 6.2 of the main text measures this.`));

